    "neg": 0
   },
   "solution": "<p>0.94 (0.90 - 0.99)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/14/144b45b8b5f028b8a733.png\" /></div>"
  },
  {
   "n": 9,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • ELECTRONICS ENGINEERING • +1 / -0",
   "html": "<p>Which of the following methods are used for the demodulation of FM signal?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "Balanced slope detector"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "Foster-Seeley Discriminator"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "Ratio Detector"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "Phase locked loop (PLL) detector"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B",
    "C",
    "D"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "A, B, C, D",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 1,
    "neg": 0
   },
   "solution": "<p>(a, b, c, d)<br />\nBalanced slope detector: This is a FM demodulator that uses two tuned LC circuits to convert<br />\nfrequency variations into amplitude variations. The two tuned circuits are connected in back-toback<br />\nfashion to opposite ends of centre-tapped transformer to provide better linearity.<br />\nFoster-seeley discriminator: This circuit uses a tuned LC circuit and diodes to produce an output<br />\nvoltage that is proportional to the frequency deviation of the FM signal.<br />\nRatio Detector: This type of discriminator also uses tuned circuits and diodes, but it&rsquo;s designed<br />\nto suppress amplitude variations in the FM signal, improving its robustness.<br />\nPLL: PLLs are feedback systems that track the frequency of the incoming FM signal. The PLL&rsquo;s<br />\noutput voltage is proportional to the instantaneous frequency of the FM signal, effectively<br />\ndemodulating it.</p>"
  },
